<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Which tags may wake NexFlow while it is closed (see NfcTagDispatchAlias in the manifest).

    Matching is counter-intuitive: a tech-list matches when it is a SUBSET of what the tag
    reports, so listing FEWER technologies matches MORE tags. Entries within one tech-list are
    ANDed; separate tech-lists are ORed. One tech per list is therefore the widest each can be.

    Ndef and NdefFormatable, and deliberately nothing else:

    - Ndef is the only technology the platform guarantees. "It is mandatory for all Android
      devices with NFC to correctly enumerate Ndef on NFC Forum Tag Types 1-4", so this list
      matches the same tags on every phone. MifareClassic and MifareUltralight are explicitly
      optional ("If it is not implemented, then MifareClassic will never be enumerated in
      getTechList") — listing those would silently never match on a non-NXP NFC chipset, with
      no error and nothing in the log.
    - It also keeps NexFlow away from the cards this was hijacking. Transit passes and door
      badges are MifareClassic, payment cards are IsoDep, Suica is NfcF — none of them enumerate
      Ndef, so none of them reach us. That is a tighter net than the TAG_DISCOVERED filter this
      replaces, which caught every tag no other app claimed.

    A tag carrying a URL still goes to the browser: NDEF_DISCOVERED outranks TECH_DISCOVERED and
    should. Blank and UID-only tags — what a NexFlow NFC trigger is actually built on — fall
    through to here.
-->
<resources xmlns:xliff="urn:oasis:names:tc:xliff:document:1.2">
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.Ndef</tech>
    </tech-list>
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.NdefFormatable</tech>
    </tech-list>
</resources>
